WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.672
This lesson takes motion one step further.

2
00:00:04.672 --> 00:00:09.343
Speed only tells how fast something is moving.

3
00:00:09.343 --> 00:00:14.015
Velocity tells how fast and in which direction.

4
00:00:14.015 --> 00:00:18.686
That means motion can keep the same speed and still have a changing velocity if the direction changes.

5
00:00:18.686 --> 00:00:23.358
Acceleration then measures how quickly that velocity changes.

6
00:00:23.358 --> 00:00:28.029
So acceleration is not only about speeding up.

7
00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:32.701
It is about any change in the velocity description, whether that change comes from speed, direction, or both.

8
00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:37.372
Keep this takeaway in view: Velocity is a direction-aware motion description, and acceleration measures how that description changes.

9
00:00:37.372 --> 00:00:42.044
Speed means how fast.

10
00:00:42.044 --> 00:00:46.715
Magnitude only.

11
00:00:46.715 --> 00:00:51.387
No direction is needed.

12
00:00:51.387 --> 00:00:56.058
Velocity means how fast + which way.

13
00:00:56.058 --> 00:01:00.730
Direction turns the motion description into a vector.

14
00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:07.347
The velocity-arrow analogy is the best way to make this lesson feel physical.

15
00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:13.963
The arrow length represents how large the velocity is.

16
00:01:13.963 --> 00:01:20.580
The arrow direction represents which way the motion points.

17
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:27.197
If the arrow grows longer, shorter, or turns, the velocity has changed.

18
00:01:27.197 --> 00:01:33.813
That is why acceleration is really the story of how the arrow changes from one moment to the next.

19
00:01:33.813 --> 00:01:40.430
Students often remember only the length change, but a turning arrow also counts as a velocity change.

20
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:47.047
Keep this takeaway in view: A turning object can have acceleration even if its speedometer stays constant, because its velocity arrow is changing direction.

21
00:01:47.047 --> 00:01:53.663
Length change: A longer or shorter arrow means the size of the velocity changed.

22
00:01:53.663 --> 00:02:00.280
Direction change: A turned arrow also means the velocity changed, even if the speed stayed the same.

23
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.026
The calculation method becomes much safer when the sign convention is chosen at the start.

24
00:02:05.026 --> 00:02:09.771
Pick one direction to be positive.

25
00:02:09.771 --> 00:02:14.517
Then write the initial and final velocities with signs.

26
00:02:14.517 --> 00:02:19.262
After that, use acceleration equals change in velocity divided by time.

27
00:02:19.262 --> 00:02:24.008
Change in velocity means final velocity minus initial velocity.

28
00:02:24.008 --> 00:02:28.754
Only after calculating should you interpret what the sign means.

29
00:02:28.754 --> 00:02:33.499
This is important because a negative acceleration sign does not automatically mean slowing down.

30
00:02:33.499 --> 00:02:38.245
It only tells you which way the acceleration points in the chosen coordinate system.

31
00:02:38.245 --> 00:02:42.991
Keep this takeaway in view: The sign convention is not decoration.

32
00:02:42.991 --> 00:02:47.736
It controls what the minus sign actually means.

33
00:02:47.736 --> 00:02:52.482
Good habit: Write east as positive or forward as positive before touching the numbers.

34
00:02:52.482 --> 00:02:57.228
Common error: Using only magnitudes, then trying to guess the sign afterwards.

35
00:02:57.228 --> 00:03:01.973
a = Δv / t.

36
00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:06.719
Δv = v - u.

37
00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:11.464
Write the velocities with signs first.

38
00:03:11.464 --> 00:03:16.210
Interpret the sign after the calculation.

39
00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:22.410
One of the most useful corrections in this lesson is that turning counts.

40
00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:28.610
Imagine a car taking a bend at constant speed.

41
00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:34.810
If the speed stays constant, some students say the acceleration is zero.

42
00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:41.010
But the direction of motion is changing all the time, which means the velocity is changing.

43
00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:47.210
Because acceleration tracks change in velocity, the acceleration is not zero.

44
00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:53.410
This idea matters because it stops acceleration from becoming a speed-only concept.

45
00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:59.610
Keep this takeaway in view: Acceleration includes direction change, not just getting faster or slower in a straight line.

46
00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:05.810
Constant speed: The speedometer reading can stay the same.

47
00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:12.010
Changing direction: The direction of motion keeps rotating as the object turns.

48
00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:16.985
Take a signed pattern now.

49
00:04:16.985 --> 00:04:21.959
Suppose east is positive.

50
00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:26.934
A cyclist slows from plus 12 metres per second to plus 4 metres per second in 2 seconds.

51
00:04:26.934 --> 00:04:31.908
The change in velocity is 4 minus 12, which is minus 8 metres per second.

52
00:04:31.908 --> 00:04:36.883
Then divide by 2 seconds to get minus 4 metres per second squared.

53
00:04:36.883 --> 00:04:41.858
The negative sign tells you the acceleration points west, opposite the chosen positive direction.

54
00:04:41.858 --> 00:04:46.832
That interpretation is much cleaner when the sign convention was chosen before the substitution.

55
00:04:46.832 --> 00:04:51.807
Keep this takeaway in view: Signed velocity change first, sign interpretation second.

56
00:04:51.807 --> 00:04:56.782
That order prevents most mistakes.

57
00:04:56.782 --> 00:05:01.756
Initial velocity means +12 m/s.

58
00:05:01.756 --> 00:05:06.731
East is positive, so eastward motion carries a plus sign.

59
00:05:06.731 --> 00:05:11.705
Final velocity means +4 m/s.

60
00:05:11.705 --> 00:05:16.680
Still eastward, but smaller in magnitude.

61
00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:22.627
This trap deserves special attention because it appears again and again.

62
00:05:22.627 --> 00:05:28.573
If the acceleration is negative, that does not automatically mean the object is slowing down.

63
00:05:28.573 --> 00:05:34.520
Whether it slows down or speeds up depends on both the velocity direction and the acceleration direction.

64
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:40.467
For example, if the motion is already negative and the acceleration is also negative, the speed can increase while the acceleration stays negative.

65
00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:46.413
So the minus sign is directional information first.

66
00:05:46.413 --> 00:05:52.360
Only after comparing it with the velocity sign can you decide what is happening to the speed.

67
00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:58.307
Keep this takeaway in view: The sign of acceleration by itself is not a speed judgment.

68
00:05:58.307 --> 00:06:04.253
It is a direction statement.

69
00:06:04.253 --> 00:06:10.200
Wrong shortcut: Negative acceleration always means slowing down.

70
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:16.147
Better statement: Negative acceleration means the acceleration points in the chosen negative direction.

71
00:06:16.147 --> 00:06:22.093
Acceleration sign -> direction information.

72
00:06:22.093 --> 00:06:28.040
Velocity sign -> current motion direction.

73
00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.890
Check three things quickly.

74
00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.740
What extra feature turns speed into velocity?

75
00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.590
Direction.

76
00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:43.440
What quantity does acceleration measure?

77
00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.290
The rate of change of velocity.

78
00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:51.140
If east is positive and the final velocity is smaller than the initial velocity while both stay eastward, what sign should the acceleration have?

79
00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:54.990
Negative.

80
00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.840
Those are the exact habits that make the worked example feel much less intimidating.

81
00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:02.690
Keep this takeaway in view: Direction, signed velocity, and change-per-time are the three anchors for this lesson.

82
00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:06.540
Speed -> velocity means direction.

83
00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.390
That extra feature changes the quantity category.

84
00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:14.240
Acceleration means rate of change.

85
00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.090
It measures how quickly the velocity changes.

86
00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:23.058
You are ready for the lesson example.

87
00:07:23.058 --> 00:07:28.026
Take east as positive.

88
00:07:28.026 --> 00:07:32.994
Write the initial velocity as plus 12 metres per second and the final velocity as plus 4 metres per second.

89
00:07:32.994 --> 00:07:37.961
Then use acceleration equals change in velocity divided by time.

90
00:07:37.961 --> 00:07:42.929
The change in velocity is 4 minus 12, which is minus 8 metres per second.

91
00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:47.897
Divide by 2 seconds to get minus 4 metres per second squared.

92
00:07:47.897 --> 00:07:52.865
Finally, interpret the sign: the acceleration points west, opposite the chosen positive direction.

93
00:07:52.865 --> 00:07:57.833
That is the whole method chain.

94
00:07:57.833 --> 00:08:02.801
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is just signed change in velocity divided by time, interpreted through the chosen direction convention.

95
00:08:02.801 --> 00:08:07.769
Step 1: Choose the positive direction first.

96
00:08:07.769 --> 00:08:12.736
Step 2: Write both velocities with signs and calculate final minus initial.

97
00:08:12.736 --> 00:08:17.704
Δv = 4 - 12 = -8 m/s.

98
00:08:17.704 --> 00:08:22.672
a = -8 / 2 = -4 m/s^2.

99
00:08:22.672 --> 00:08:27.640
The negative sign is interpreted using the sign convention chosen at the start.
